<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    This is the DEVICE entitlements file. It holds no
    com.apple.security.application-groups key. A free Apple team grants no App
    Group, so a sideload profile from AltStore or SideStore lacks the key. An
    .ipa that requests it fails to install.

    ponytail: the App Group IS what the iOS 26 SIMULATOR keychain needed.
    Measured on the iPhone 17 simulator with an adhoc "Sign to Run Locally"
    signature:
      - an empty entitlements file launches and SecItemCopyMatching returns
        -34018 (errSecMissingEntitlement);
      - keychain-access-groups SIGKILLs the app at exec and the launch is
        denied by SBMainWorkspace, with or without a team prefix, with or
        without application-identifier and com.apple.developer.team-identifier;
      - only com.apple.security.application-groups makes the read succeed.
    So the simulator build takes the App Group from Daak.simulator.entitlements,
    selected with CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS[sdk=iphonesimulator*]. THIS file stays
    clear of the group so the DEVICE build still installs from a free profile.

    ponytail: the removal parks three features on a DEVICE build. They are:
      1. the iOS share sheet (the DaakShareExtension.appex is not embedded);
      2. the Siri and Shortcuts send (DaakIntentBridge);
      3. the notification-action stash (DaakNotifyActionStash).
    The three Swift call sites fail soft on a nil container. The app still runs.

    To re-enable the three features on a DEVICE build:
      1. restore this key in Daak.entitlements;
      2. restore this key in DaakShareExtension.entitlements;
      3. restore the DaakShareExtension.appex entry in the
         "Embed App Extensions" build phase of Daak.xcodeproj/project.pbxproj;
      4. pay for the Apple Developer Program, which grants the App Group.
  -->
</dict>
</plist>
